      "text": "To find \\(\\sin F\\) in the right triangle \\(DEF\\), we need to first understand the given information and then relate it to the trigonometric definitions of the angles in the triangle.\n\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\sin D = \\frac{5}{13}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sin E = 1\n\\]\n\nFirst, we need to identify the type of triangle and the angles. Since \\(\\sin E = 1\\), angle \\(E\\) must be \\(90^\\circ\\) (or \\(0^\\circ\\)), which makes triangle \\(DEF\\) a right triangle with \\(\\angle E = 90^\\circ\\). This means \\(\\angle F = 90^\\circ - \\angle D\\).\n\nNext, we know that in a right triangle:\n\\[\n\\sin F = \\cos D\n\\]\nbecause \\(\\angle D\\) is adjacent to \\(\\angle F\\) and the hypotenuse of the triangle is the same.\n\nGiven \\(\\sin D = \\frac{5}{13}\\), and using the Pythagorean theorem for the right triangle, we can find \\(\\cos D\\). The hypotenuse of the triangle is 13 units (since \\(\\sin D = \\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} = \\frac{5}{13}\\)). Let the adjacent side to angle \\(D\\) be 5 units. Using the Pythagorean theorem:\n\\[\n13^2 = 5^2 + a^2\n\\]\n\\[\n169 = 25 + a^2\n\\]\n\\[\na^2 = 144\n\\]\n\\[\na = 12\n\\]\nThis means the adjacent side to angle \\(E\\) is 5 units and the opposite side is 12 units. Since \\(\\sin D = \\frac{5}{13}\\) and \\(\\sin E = 1\\) (which is opposite the right angle), \\(\\cos D\\) is the adjacent side to angle \\(D\\), which is 5 units.\n\nThus, \\(\\sin F = \\cos D = 5/13\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{6}{14}} \\implies \\boxed{\\frac{3}{7}}\n\\]",
